
//application level virtualisation 
    Virtualizes only a single application, not the full operating system or hardware.
    The application runs as if it’s installed on the native system, even if the underlying OS or environment is different.

    how it works 
     Only the app’s environment is virtualized (libraries, runtime dependencies).
     The rest of the OS and hardware are used normally.
     App runs independently of the local desktop.
     No need to actually install the app in the host system.
     Application can run on an OS that does not natively support it.

  emulation :   lets a program compiled for one hardware architecture run on a completely different hardware architecture.
  techniques 
  1. interpretation 
     Each instruction from the original program is interpreted one by one.
     minimal cost but very slow 
  2. binary transaltion 
       Original instructions are translated into native instructions.
       Translated blocks are cached and reused, so repeated execution is fast.
    
  Emulation = only executes a program compiled for a different hardware.
  Hardware virtualization = emulates full hardware environment, so an entire OS can run.
 
 Application virtualization allows you to run an app on a system 
 without actually installing it on that system — and without worrying about OS compatibility or missing libraries.

