//storage virtualisation 
   Storage virtualization is a technique that decouples the physical storage hardware from its logical representation.
   Users see a single logical storage system, while the data may reside across multiple physical devices.

   how it works 
   1.logical view :
         Users access storage using a logical path, not worrying about where the data physically resides.
   2. pooling combines multiple storage devices into a single virtual storage pool 
   3. single interface to manage storage 
   4. Network-Based Virtualization: Storage Area Networks (SANs) provide centralized, network-accessible storage over high-speed connections.
   
   Think of storage like a library system:
        Physical books are scattered in multiple branches (physical storage).
        Virtualization = a single online catalog (logical view) where users don’t need to know which branch holds the book.

 Storage virtualization = hide physical complexity, provide a single logical storage system, and simplify management while improving utilization.
  Storage virtualization hides the actual physical storage (hard drives, SSDs, etc.)
  and gives you a single, logical view of all your storage — no need to care where data is really stored.


//memory virtualisation 
   Memory virtualization allows guest OSes and their apps to use memory without knowing the actual physical memory layout.
   It introduces an extra layer of address translation so multiple VMs can share the same physical RAM safely.

   virtual to physical address mapping 
   virtual address (guest app) -> physical address(guest os) ->machine address(actual hardware)
   
   guest page table: virtual addresses to physical addresses 
   hypervisor table : physical addresses -> machine address 

//guest os updates its page tables without hypervisor knowing so we manintain 
  shadow page table by hyperviosr mark guest page table as read only 
  if guest ries to write -> exception is trapped 

    Guest page table:       VA → PA
  Hypervisor mapping:      PA → MA
  --------------------------------------
  Shadow page table:       VA → MA  (single table for MMU)


/page faults in virtualized memory 
    1.true miss -> mapping does not exist in guest page table -> forward page fault to guest os 
    2. hidden miss -> mapping exists in guest page table but missing in shadow  table -> hypervisor silently updates shadow table 

//shadow page tables 
    pros -> guest os code can execute directly on cpu suign VA ->MA mapping 
    but requires double memory usage(guest table + shadow table ),overhead from hypervisor traps 


//to reduce overhead 
    second level address translation (SLAT) 
    intel : extended page tables (EPT),AMD : nested page tables(NPT)
    into one multilevel table -> reduces hypervisor overhead 
    uses improved TLBs to speed up address translation 
    tagged TLB  is better ,tlb is modified to reduce miss rate 
    large tlb- more expensive , tagged tlb - every entry in tlb has an address space identifier 

summary 
    Memory virtualization = VA → PA → MA mapping.
    Shadow page tables allow direct translation but add overhead. (VA ->MA)
    SLAT (EPT/NPT) reduces overhead by combining page tables in hardware.

  Memory virtualization makes each program think it has its own private memory,
    even though it’s actually sharing the physical RAM with other programs or virtual machines.


//network virtualisation 
   Creating virtual versions of physical network components like switches, routers, and interfaces, so that:
     Multiple networks can be combined into one logical network.
     one physical network can be split into many virtual ones.
  
     It uses software + hardware to manage virtual networks that behave like real ones.
     types :
    1. external network virtualization 
       Combines multiple physical networks into one logical network.
       Used to group computers together virtually.
       ex: use vlans to connect multiple devices into same logical 
    2. internal network virtualisation 
       Used inside a physical server, often with VMs or containers.
       Each VM or container gets a virtual network interface.

 network virtualization puts your network into software —
 so you can manage, divide, and control it easily without changing cables or hardware.

//desktop virtualisation 
The desktop environment (your personal computer screen, apps, files, etc.) is not running on your physical device.
Instead, it is hosted on a remote server or data center.
You just connect to it from anywhere using a thin client, web browser, or remote desktop software.

Instead of carrying your laptop around, you log in to your desktop remotely — and it looks and works exactly like your own personal computer.

types of desktop virtualisation 

| Type                                     | Description                                                                                    |
| ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **VDI (Virtual Desktop Infrastructure)** | Each user gets their own **virtual machine** with their own desktop OS. High customization.    |
| **RDS (Remote Desktop Services)**        | Users **share a single Windows server** with multiple sessions. Less isolated.                 |
| **DaaS (Desktop as a Service)**          | VDI hosted on **cloud providers** like AWS WorkSpaces, Azure Virtual Desktop. Managed for you. |


//live migration adn server consolidation 
  Live Migration is the process of moving a running virtual machine (VM) 
  from one physical server to another without shutting it down, so that the services continue uninterrupted.

  how it works 
  1. hypervisor copies vm memory adn cpu state to the destination server while teh vm keeps running 
  2. Synchronizes changes made during the copying.
  3. Switches execution to the new server.
  4. Old server stops running the VM.

  benefits 
  .reduce downtime 
  balance load across servers 
  high availability 
  fautl tolerance 

  //server consolidation 
    Server Consolidation is the practice of combining workloads from multiple underutilized servers onto fewer, more powerful servers using virtualization.
    By moving multiple VMs onto fewer servers, you save space, energy, and management costs.

    how it works 
    1.identify underutilized servers 
    2. create vms for their workloads 
    3. move vms onto fewer physical servers 
    4. turn off old servers 

Live Migration is often used during server consolidation to move VMs safely from old or low-utilization servers to consolidated servers without downtime.

1.precopy migration 
    Pre-Copy moves the VM memory pages to the destination server before stopping the VM, while the VM keeps running on the source.

    how it works 
    .copy memory pages from source to destination while vm is running 
    . track modiefied pages(dirty pages) during copying 
    . repeat copying dirty pages until only a small number remain 
    . stop the vm ,copy the last few pages then resume vm from destination 

    very low downtime 
2.Postcopy migration 
    Post-Copy starts the VM on the destination immediately and fetches memory pages from the source as needed.

    how it works 
    . stop the vm at the source 
    . start the vm on destination immediately 
    . fetch memory pages on demand from source 
    . remaining pages are sent in backgroudn 

    slowdowns -> while rendering the pages 

//xen hypervisor 
   Xen is an open-source type-1 (bare-metal) hypervisor that allows multiple operating systems (guests) 
    to run on the same physical hardware simultaneously.
 Type-1 hypervisor → runs directly on hardware, not on top of another OS.
 Designed for high performance and isolation.
 
  Type: Open-source virtualization platform based on paravirtualization.
  Guest OS interacts with hypervisor via paravirtualization, meaning the OS is aware it's running on a virtualized environment.

//VMWARE  sgx server 
server virtualisation 

1. serverd (daemon process)
   It is a background process.
   Controls and manages all VMware application processes.
   Acts like the central controller.
2. VMware driver
  Installed on the host OS.
  Connects VMware applications to actual virtual machines.
  Works like a bridge between the VMware apps and the VM hardware.
3. VMM (Virtual Machine Monitor)
   Manages each virtual machine instance.
   Basically runs the virtual machine.
4. User requests
   Users send VM create/start/stop requests through a Web server.
   These requests go to serverd.
    serverd forwards them to the VMM.
    VMM performs the required VM operations.

serverd controls VMware apps → VMware driver links apps to VMs → VMM manages VMs → user requests go from Web server → serverd → VMM.

