| **Type of Virtualization**            | **Definition (Simple)**                                                                    | **Examples**                           |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| **1. Application Virtualization**     | Virtualizes only the application; runs as if installed locally even when it’s not.         | Wine, VMware ThinApp, Microsoft App-V  |
| **2. OS-Level Virtualization**        | Multiple isolated containers share the same OS kernel.                                     | Docker, LXC, OpenVZ                    |
| **3. Hardware / Full Virtualization** | Entire hardware is emulated so unmodified OS can run as a VM.                              | VMware ESXi, VirtualBox, Hyper-V, KVM  |
| **4. Paravirtualization**             | Guest OS is modified to interact with hypervisor for faster performance.                   | Xen (PV mode)                          |
| **5. Storage Virtualization**         | Combines many physical disks into one logical storage system.                              | SAN, RAID, vSAN                        |
| **6. Memory Virtualization**          | Virtual memory abstraction allowing VMs to have their own memory; uses SLAT/shadow paging. | Shadow Page Tables, Intel EPT, AMD NPT |
| **7. Network Virtualization**         | Creates virtual networks independent of physical hardware (VLANs, virtual NICs).           | VLAN, Virtual Switch, NAT              |
| **8. Desktop Virtualization**         | Desktop environment runs on a remote server; accessed from anywhere.                       | VDI, RDS, DaaS                         |
| **9. Server Virtualization**          | One physical server is divided into multiple virtual servers.                              | VMware ESXi, XenServer, Hyper-V        |
| **10. Data Virtualization**           | Combines data from multiple sources and shows it as a single view.                         | Denodo, Tibco Data Virtualization      |
| **11. GPU Virtualization**            | Shares a single physical GPU across multiple VMs/containers.                               | NVIDIA vGPU, AMD MxGPU                 |
| **12. Application Containerization**  | Packages apps + dependencies in isolated containers.                                       | Docker, Kubernetes Pods                |

